"A hiccup is a mistake in the rhythm. Your diaphragm suddenly jerks down, and air rushes in. A tiny moment later, the gate in your throat slams shut, and the air hitting it makes the sound: hic! Eating too fast or drinking soda can start hiccups. Babies even hiccup before they are born. Nobody is sure what hiccups are for.": "e5adfbfeec3c",
"A reflex needs no thinking. A message travels along a nerve to the brainstem, the stalk at the bottom of your brain. The brainstem answers at once by sending orders to your muscles. It is the same part that keeps you breathing all night. Most of the muscles in this lesson are breathing muscles, and the biggest is the diaphragm, a dome of muscle under your lungs. When it pulls down, air rushes in.": "1652fcbb7777",
"A sneeze is a cleaning job. When dust or pollen tickles the inside of your nose, nerve endings there send a message to the brainstem. You take a deep breath, a gate in your throat shuts for an instant, your chest squeezes, and then the air blasts out, carrying the dust with it. One sneeze can spray about 40,000 tiny droplets, and they can fly several meters. That is why you cover a sneeze with your elbow or a tissue.": "877dd59dcef1",
"A sudden noise made us jump.": "9689a59f6cd8",
"A yawn is the slow one. Your jaw opens wide, you take a long breath in, your muscles stretch, and then you breathe out fast. People once thought a yawn brings in extra oxygen, but tests do not support that idea. It may help to cool the brain or to wake you up. And yawns are catching! From about the age of six, seeing someone yawn can make you yawn too. Dogs and chimpanzees catch yawns as well.": "5a98245ae8bb",
"Blinking is a reflex.": "1fccc5b0c7c2",
"Cover your mouth when you cough.": "4befe7e0a7bb",
"Here is one more reflex, and you are doing it right now: blinking. Each blink spreads tears across your eye and wipes it clean, and it lasts only about a tenth of a second. A grown-up blinks about 17 times a minute. But when people read or stare at a screen, they blink only 4 or 5 times a minute, and their eyes get dry and tired. Try the blink counter below the model with a partner.": "c7b53c42b59f",
"I got the hiccups after drinking soda.": "a55fec58927a",
"May I have a tissue, please?": "78a64a27ed1b",
"Pepper makes me sneeze.": "e05d0821490b",
"The sleepy cat began to yawn.": "4712513ba8e9",
"What happens in your body when you hiccup?": "3a8f0ad79e19",
"What is a reflex?": "657f63ac4b68",
"Why do your eyes get tired when you look at a screen for a long time?": "df304d509aa0",
"You are sitting quietly in class when, without warning: Achoo! You did not decide to sneeze. Your body did it for you. A sneeze, a hiccup, and a yawn are all things your body does on its own. Actions like these are called reflexes. Why does your body have them, and how do they work?": "3c4f75dd475e",
